{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310656,"nonce":"+bEdTcEhVDSJy9EJE3n8V2ehYYA=","title":"FIXED bab87ce0 — the module-count SIGSEGV was TWO id-space collisions: ctor pun (SymbolId-as-TypeId) bound new ChatResponse to ...","message":"Found 2026-07-28 adding the decode-split profiler. A new\n`nue.transformer.DecodeProfile` **SIGSEGVs the x86 build mid-generation**\n(exit 139, \"core dumped\", no panic text, no diagnostic). **macOS runs fine.**\nReproducible: commit `35952bdf` segfaults on every run; HEAD (`9ae4816b`) does not.\n\n\n**gdb was the pivot**: `haxe_string_compare` with `rdi = 151645 = the EOS id`,\nframe #1 `ask` — an Int where a `HaxeString*` belongs. Adding DecodeProfile\nshifted symbol numbering so **ChatResponse's class SymbolId became 650** on\nx86, colliding twice:\n\n1. **Ctor pun.** `new ChatResponse` → the New lowering's fallback does\n   `constructor_map.get(TypeId::from_raw(sym.as_raw()))` — SymbolId reinterpreted\n   as TypeId, two numbering spaces in one map. TypeId(650) held GenerationLoop's\n   ctor (cached there by the earlier `new GenerationLoop` in the same fn).\n   Probe: `[CTOR] class=ChatResponse path=SYMBOL-PUN ... fid=640008`.\n   FIX: `constructor_owner_map` (fid→owning class, recorded at registration and\n   from import keys); the pun accepts only owner-matching candidates. The pun is\n   load-bearing for ~34 self-referential sites — do NOT delete it.\n2. **Field candidates compare CONTEXT-LOCAL TypeIds.** `loop.finishReason`\n   (GenerationLoop slot 7 String): the direct SymbolId lookup was RIGHT, but the\n   \"drift-proof\" name+receiver probe OVERRODE it — ChatResponse's field entry\n   carries class_ty=TypeId(650) minted in ITS module, which equals\n   GenerationLoop's TypeId in Conversation's context → `Unique(650, 4)` → reads\n   the eos Int slot as a String. Probe: `[FLD] ... direct=(3835,7)\n   probe=unique chosen=(650,4)`. FIX: Strategy 0 in\n   `resolve_field_index_candidates` — a SINGLE candidate whose recorded owner\n   (`field_class_names`, session-global SymbolId key) matches the receiver's\n   class wins before any TypeId comparison. Positive-selection only; inherited\n   fields (owner=parent name) fall through unchanged.\n\n**THE RULE: TypeIds are context-local. Only SymbolIds and names may cross a\nmodule boundary.** Any map that mixes TypeIds from different modules, or puns\nSymbolId↔TypeId, will produce host-dependent mis-binding as module counts\nshift. Remaining known puns: hir_to_mir.rs:1614/2129 use the same\n`TypeId::from_raw(sym)` idiom — deliberate dual-keying (\"Class runtime type_id\n= SymbolId.as_raw()\"), same latent risk, unaudited.\n\nmacOS \"worked\" only because ChatResponse's local TypeId there (655) collided\nwith nothing. Same source, same compiler — different readdir order, different\nnumbering, different winner.\n\nstatic-only module / static→static / inliner-func_id-not-remapped (real gap in\n`remap_instruction` but every id resolved — 0 dangling, all fn-ptrs real) /\nnull-compare lowering (byte-identical in working code) / undeclared `fn*0000`\nids (= each module's malloc, normal). SIX bisect steps and FOUR delta-repros\nall pointed at \"the module's existence\" — the module's existence merely shifted\nthe NUMBERING. **A bisect on a numbering-sensitive bug identifies the\nperturbation, not the defect. gdb registers + resolved MIR named it.**\n\nHost the statics on a class the graph already instantiates and already calls\ncross-module — `nue.Q4Matmul`. Its `splitNow` was NOT inlined, so the call\nstayed a proper cross-module call and no foreign ids were copied.\n`GQAttention.dumpAttnProfile()` is fine for the same reason.\n\n| # | change | result |\n|---|--------|--------|\n| 1 | drop the `LlamaModel` → cross-PACKAGE call | still segv |\n| 2 | drop `x.shape()[0]` | still segv |\n| 3 | drop `noteBlock`, keep `now()` | still segv |\n| 4 | inline `enabled()` (no static→static) | still segv |\n| 5 | strip every reference from `LlamaBlock` | still segv |\n| 6 | strip the last reference (`Main.hx`) | RUNS |\n\n**Step 5/6 made me conclude \"a class whose ONLY use is a cross-module static\ncall cannot survive the module graph\". THAT WAS WRONG** — a faithful\nreconstruction as `nue.transformer.TinyStatic` (same fields, env read,\nstatic→static calls, uncalled statics, the full 20-operand concat chain, called\nonce from Main) **runs clean**. Four escalating delta-repros against the real\nnue tree all passed. Only the MIR diff found it.\n\n**Lesson: when a bisect's last step \"fixes\" it, that identifies a *trigger*,\nnot a *cause*.** Removing the last reference also removed the inlining\nopportunity. Diff the MIR of a known-good against a known-bad build before\nbelieving a bisect's story.\n\nStandalone repros (2-file, and package-nested static-only alongside a used\nclass) also run clean — the cheap repro LIES here, unlike\n[[bugs_static_to_static_call_traps_in_import_module]] where it was the win.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_new_module_static_only_sigsegv` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}